// An #analog reading is SIGNED by default -- the same rule a #variable has.
//
// The value slot is `unsigned val:16` and cannot hold a negative, so a signed
// analog stores the low 16 bits and gets them sign-extended on the way out.
// `unsigned` on the declaration is the opt-in for the raw 16-bit reading, which
// is what a packed word (an RGB565 pixel) wants rather than a quantity.
//
// The declaration's vt used to be dropped entirely on this path: `signed`
// parsed and meant nothing at all.

#analog Sg:16 inout 0:2            // signed by default
#analog Un:16 unsigned inout 0:3

#variable a = 0
#variable b = 0

// 0xF000 has bit 15 set. Signed it is -4096; unsigned it is 61440.
Sg = 0xF000 ? 1
Un = 0xF000 ? 1

a = Sg ? 1
b = Un ? 1
